# Content and schema documents are compared byte for byte.
#
# The canonical form these files are committed in is defined down to the byte —
# member order, indentation, escaping, and a bare newline — because the API
# exports what the site's contributors publish back into this tree, and an
# export whose only difference from the checked-in file is the line ending
# produces a pull request touching all 7,877 documents.
#
# Left to `core.autocrlf`, a checkout on Windows would hand the exporter and its
# round-trip tests CRLF files and the same command would produce a clean tree on
# one machine and a dirty one on another. Pinning the working tree to LF makes
# the format the same everywhere, which is the whole point of having one.
*.json text eol=lf

# The publisher runs as the image's entrypoint, so its line endings are not a
# matter of taste. `* text=auto` below normalises on the way in, which keeps the
# committed file LF and is why images built in CI have always worked — but it
# leaves the working tree to the platform, and on Windows that means CRLF. A
# `docker build` from such a checkout copies the file in verbatim and produces a
# container that dies on start with
#
#   exec /usr/local/bin/sw5e-publish-content: no such file or directory
#
# which names the interpreter rather than the carriage return that broke it, and
# reads exactly like a missing file. Pinning the working tree to LF makes a local
# build behave the same as CI.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Everything else follows git's own detection, with text normalised on the way
# in so a contributor's editor cannot commit CRLF by accident.
* text=auto
